      {
        "id": 138935,
        "name": "kreistor",
        "username": "kreistor",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/k/9e8a1a/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-04-10T09:18:03.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>There are two parts to Frost's psychology. the second is the accident. The first is the loss of his fiance to a drunk driver, which he blames himself for.</p><p>Blade has little chance of converting Frost to fight law and order. It was criminal behavior that cost him most. As much as Frost would prefer not to fight, he would not turn to side with criminals just to increase his personal welath or other gains. He wouldn't want to cause anyone else to lose someone the way he did. even if he has less conscience than we might desire in a hero, it would be a betrayal of her memory to turn against society.</p><p>As for the nemesis, that was Blade. Proletariat was inspired to hate AZ by Blade's twisted logic, so Frost's choice in that enmity was very limited. Proletariat was inspired to choose Frost as an enemy, rather than a mutual event.</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
        "post_type": 1,
